1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A stock split should never liquidate a healthy borrower. But when lending protocols read raw token balances, it can.

2
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:22,030
Meet CorpShift: the corporate action runtime for onchain finance. In our live Protocol Lab, two vaults begin with the same position: one thousand dollars of collateral, four hundred dollars of debt, and a health factor of two.

3
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:29,580
We attest a four for one split. Before execution, CorpShift blocks new borrowing while the action is pending.

4
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:36,970
Then the token multiplier moves to four, and the per share price falls from one hundred dollars to twenty five.

5
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:42,440
The naive vault now sees only two hundred fifty dollars of collateral and liquidates.

6
00:00:42,790 --> 00:00:50,870
The CorpShift aware vault reads forty economic units, keeps the correct one thousand dollar value, and protects the position.

7
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:58,740
Same user. Same chain. Same event. Two radically different outcomes. Every accepted write has a transaction receipt.

8
00:00:59,090 --> 00:01:08,610
Every rejected operation is checked against the expected contract error. CorpShift keeps stock token finance economically correct when the underlying stock changes.
